## Decision and scope

Track two problems within [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471): a shared date parser defect and an incorrect booking-v3 date-field hint. The course accepted these two tickets. A trial grouping into one ticket was rejected because it combined two problems. The same visible day/month swap can arise from different input contracts.

## Engineering update — 5 October

The [course desk update](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows#engineering-update), checked on 28 October after ticket acceptance, reports that #4471 closed on 5 October:

- **Parser: fixed.** Engineering change: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Public ticket #1 is closed as completed.
- **Hint: won't fix.** The booking-v3 form was retired. Public ticket #2 is closed as not planned; retirement is not a hint correction.

This update supersedes the export's known/queued state. It reports engineering's disposition; we did not independently run the production system. The report counts below remain the historical export through 4 October, not new live queue counts. These findings concern `sched-prod`, not the desk's other products.

## Problem tickets and report coverage

<IncidentReview />

For the shared input path, the tenant enters a day-first date and month-first interpretation swaps it. For booking-v3, the month-first hint prompts input that the form interprets in tenant day-first order. Both can turn an intended 7 April into 4 July; both contracts must be made consistent.

The desk noted five unusual descriptions, including one-day moves and an invite arriving before booking. Preserve them in the incident and investigate their original timestamps and invite data. Do not infer a separate root cause or prescribe a timezone fix from wording alone.

## Queue history

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports began in April; merging into the umbrella ticket began in July. The merge spike reflects accumulated reports being filed, not necessarily a new spike in failures. Merging continued afterwards. A known label or queued fix does not establish resolution.

## Proposed replacement for subject-only triage

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) remains unchanged as accepted history. This record proposes its replacement; the desk must adopt the rule before practice changes.

1. Read each body and establish the product, intended date, observed date, tenant locale, booking form and channel.
2. Check tenant settings. Browser locale and intake tags are clues, not reliable cause labels.
3. Use evidence to choose the incident boundary. Matching subjects alone are insufficient. File separate tickets for different demonstrated mechanisms, even when they cause the same visible symptom.
4. Preserve unusual symptoms and missing facts for investigation. Do not manufacture a new root cause from a different description. The desk-noted timing reports remain footnoted under their tenant-based cause.
5. Deduplicate exports by report ID. Each report counts once, even if its ticket or task assignment changes.
6. Review new reports, update counts and links, and verify every affected path before closing either problem ticket. Re-read stale guidance against current evidence.

## What the existing explorer establishes

<DedupeExplorer />

The explorer compares the source classifier's causes. It shows that equal subjects can conceal different problems and different subjects can share a cause. Subject alone is therefore insufficient for deduplication. An unknown tenant requires more information.

## Handoff and verification

[Parser ticket #1](https://github.com/skarlark/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) and [form-hint ticket #2](https://github.com/skarlark/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2) each contain their report IDs and independent acceptance criteria. The earlier timing ticket was deleted with the student's approval after its evidence was preserved.

Validate tenant date order on app/email and booking-v2 paths. Validate the booking-v3 hint, validation and interpretation together on all three affected tenants. Check the generated invite's intended day and month in each case. For unusual timing reports, collect original invites, event and recipient timezones, stored timestamps and creation/delivery logs. Engineering reports the dispositions above. For independent validation, check deployment and reproduction evidence; the historical export alone contains no such confirmation.

